@meta
id: 11-08
title: Semicolon does it again for finds
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat
par: 24
minutes: 5
vscode: click twice in a long line and hope you hit the right one
open: refund.ts

@brief
One long signature, four default values in it. Finance want two of them
changed:

    currency   'GBP'  ->  'EUR'
    retries    3      ->  5

Both edits sit just past an `=`, and there are four of those on the line.
`f=` gets you to the first. The question is how you get to the third and
then the fourth without counting them by eye and without going back to
the start of the line.

`.` repeats the last change. There is a separate key that repeats the
last `f`, `t`, `F` or `T` you made, and it is worth as much.

@keys
| `f=` | forward to the next `=` |
| `;` | repeat that find, in the same direction |
| `,` | repeat it backwards — for when `;` goes one too far |
| `3;` | repeat the find three times |
| `ci'` | change inside the quotes |
| `r5` | replace the character under the cursor with `5` |

@hints
Do not count the equals signs and do not click. Land on the first one and
then walk.
--
`;` repeats the last `f`. It keeps working after you have edited
something, because an edit does not change what vim last searched for on
the line.
--
`f=` then `;;` puts you at the currency default. Change it, then a single
`;` carries on to the retries default from wherever the edit left you.

@solution
    f= ;; w ci'EUR<Esc>   ;  w r5

Two searches typed, four hops made.

`f`, `t`, `F` and `T` all leave their target behind in a small memory of
their own, and `;` re-runs it while `,` runs it the other way. That memory
is completely separate from the one `.` uses, so the two never interfere:
an edit does not cost you your find, and a find does not cost you your
repeat. In the next lesson that independence turns into a two-key loop
that eats a whole line.

`;` also takes a count, so `f=3;` would have got you to the fourth `=` in
one go. Use the count when you can see the number at a glance and `;` when
you cannot. Miscounting costs you an undo; overshooting with `;` costs you
a `,`.

@start refund.ts
export function buildRefund(orderId = '', amount = 0, currency = 'GBP', retries = 3) {
  return { orderId, amount, currency, retries };
}

@expected refund.ts
export function buildRefund(orderId = '', amount = 0, currency = 'EUR', retries = 5) {
  return { orderId, amount, currency, retries };
}
